From 768b610e9b5399ac093da86059c58216c2becfb4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vishal Kumar Swain <vkswain50@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:04:16 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] neurosym: don't fall through to a dead model solver in
 get_bv/l_get

get_bv()/l_get() called ensure_model_prog_ready() on a local_model miss
and then unconditionally fell through to smtlib_convt::get_bv()/l_get(),
which sends a (get-value) query to the interactive model solver (emit_proc)
and blocks reading its response.

ensure_model_prog_ready() can return having started nothing: under
--result-only with no --neurosym-model-prog configured, it stays silent
on purpose rather than aborting, since dec_solve() -- its only caller at
the time it was written -- just discards the result either way. What was
missed is that error_trace() (bmc.cpp) walks the whole trace and calls
get_bv()/l_get() for every variable even under --result-only (that flag
only suppresses printing the trace, not building it), so this path is
reached for real, not just in theory.

Confirmed directly: running ESBMC with --neurosym and no
--neurosym-model-prog at all, satisfiable, --result-only, on a real
40MB/998-VCC captured formula reproduced a genuine multi-hour hang (5+
hours, process alive with near-zero CPU, main thread parked in
futex_wait_queue_me) -- not a slow solve, a stuck read on a pipe with no
writer, since emit_proc was never started.

Fix: get_bv()/l_get() now check emit_proc themselves after
ensure_model_prog_ready() returns; if it is still unusable, return a
default (BigInt(0) / TV_UNKNOWN) instead of querying it. The value
returned along this path is never actually consulted -- report_trace()
only builds this trace to have something to feed the (suppressed)
printer under --result-only -- so returning a placeholder instead of
hanging is correct, not just safe. Verified: the same command now
completes in ~354s (NeuroSym's own solve time, unchanged) and prints
VERIFICATION FAILED as expected, with --neurosym-model-prog never
started.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/solvers/neurosym/neurosym_conv.cpp | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/solvers/neurosym/neurosym_conv.cpp b/src/solvers/neurosym/neurosym_conv.cpp
index 8277d9a0a9..ba053e2c6b 100644
--- a/src/solvers/neurosym/neurosym_conv.cpp
+++ b/src/solvers/neurosym/neurosym_conv.cpp
@@ -404,6 +404,20 @@ tvt neurosym_convt::l_get(smt_astt a)
     // its value (e.g. the "(- N)" form) -- treat it the same as a miss.
   }
   ensure_model_prog_ready();
+  /* ensure_model_prog_ready() can return having done nothing -- under
+   * --result-only with no --neurosym-model-prog, it stays silent instead of
+   * aborting, since dec_solve() itself only calls it to decide whether to
+   * bail out, and never touches its result otherwise. But error_trace()
+   * still walks the whole trace and calls l_get()/get_bv() for every
+   * variable even under --result-only (only the final printing is
+   * suppressed), so this path is reached for real. Falling through to
+   * smtlib_convt::l_get() here would send a (get-value) query to emit_proc
+   * and block reading a response from a solver that was never started --
+   * confirmed as a genuine multi-hour hang, not a slow solve. The trace
+   * built along this path is never printed, so the exact value returned
+   * does not matter; unknown is honest about not having one. */
+  if (!emit_proc)
+    return tvt(tvt::TV_UNKNOWN);
   return smtlib_convt::l_get(a);
 }
 
@@ -418,6 +432,11 @@ BigInt neurosym_convt::get_bv(smt_astt a, bool is_signed)
       return m;
   }
   ensure_model_prog_ready();
+  // See the matching comment in l_get(): without a usable emit_proc this
+  // would otherwise block forever reading a (get-value) response from a
+  // solver that was never started.
+  if (!emit_proc)
+    return BigInt(0);
   return smtlib_convt::get_bv(a, is_signed);
 }
 
